---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Heading from '../components/Heading.astro';
import { byDrawer, personal, stats } from '../lib/divisions.ts';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');

/** Disciplines a drawer can hold. Each of those holds nine sub-disciplines. */
const PER_DRAWER = 99;

/**
 * The room each drawer still has, counted rather than claimed.
 *
 * Two digits for the discipline is the scheme's central trade, and the only
 * honest way to argue it is to show what is still unspent after a full
 * canonical cut has been made.
 *
 * Deliberately counts disciplines only. A drawer-wide sub-discipline capacity
 * runs to hundreds and reads as reassuring, but no filer can reach it: the nine
 * you can actually use sit under a discipline you already have. It flatters the
 * scheme instead of informing the reader, so the section states the nine and
 * leaves the product out.
 */
const room = byDrawer('core').map(({ drawer, rows }) => ({
  code: drawer.code,
  name: drawer.name,
  used: rows.length,
  free: PER_DRAWER - rows.length,
}));

const totalFree = room.reduce((n, r) => n + r.free, 0);
const spentShare = Math.round((stats.divisions / (room.length * PER_DRAWER)) * 100);
---

<Base
  title="Extending the core"
  description="How to adopt the core list as it is, and how to earn a row of your own — the units digit, the three-card test, the scope-note recipe, and what never to renumber."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">The method</p>
    <h1>Extending the core</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      The <a href={`${base}/core`}>core list</a> is {stats.divisions} disciplines chosen to be
      defensible to a stranger. Your box will need rows that are defensible only to you — and
      the scheme is built so those two facts never have to fight. This is how to add yours
      without moving anything canonical.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="callout">
    <p><strong>The whole method, in four lines.</strong></p>
    <ol class="tldr">
      <li>Cut the {stats.divisions} core tabs and file against them for a few hundred cards.</li>
      <li>When a card does not fit, write the near-miss <em>on the card</em> and file it anyway.</li>
      <li>When three or four cards carry the same complaint, that is a row you have earned.</li>
      <li>Take the next free units digit under its parent. Never touch the first three.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <Heading level={2} id="as-is">First, use it exactly as it is</Heading>
  <p>
    The strong temptation on day one is to personalise the table before a single card is in the
    box — to delete the disciplines you will never write about, and to add the ones you know you
    care about. Resist both halves.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Deleting costs you nothing and gains you nothing.</strong> An unused discipline is a
    code you never type. It occupies no drawer space, because guide cards are cut for rows you
    actually file into, not for rows that exist in a table. Leaving <span class="ad">3030</span>
    in the list costs you exactly one line of a web page; deleting it costs you the day, two
    years from now, when you read something about Saturn's rings.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Adding early costs you a great deal.</strong> A tab you cut in advance is a
    prediction about which subjects will accumulate cards, and predictions about your own future
    interests are reliably wrong. What you get is a box of confident-looking tabs with two cards
    behind each, and the real filing decision — which of these near-identical rows — made harder
    rather than easier.
  </p>
  <p>
    So: cut the core tabs, file for a few weeks, and let the table be wrong at you. The
    complaints it generates are the data you need, and you cannot get them any other way.
  </p>

  <Heading level={2} id="hub">Then check that you want a discipline at all</Heading>
  <p>
    Most of the time the feeling <em>there is no row for what I care about</em> is correct and
    the conclusion is wrong. A discipline is a field of study; what people actually care about
    is usually a practice, and a practice cuts across four or five fields at once.
  </p>
  <p>
    Persuasion is not a discipline — it is rhetoric and psychology and advertising and negotiation,
    and no single row can hold it without becoming a second card box. The answer there is a
    <a href={`${base}/hubs`}>hub card</a>: one card, filed at the theme's centre of gravity,
    listing the addresses its material actually sits at. A hub costs one card and no taxonomy.
  </p>
  <div class="test">
    <p>
      <strong>The test:</strong> write down what you would file in the new discipline. If the
      list reads like one subject seen from one angle, it is a discipline. If it reads like a
      project —
      several subjects pulled together because <em>you</em> are working on something — it is a
      hub.
    </p>
  </div>

  <Heading level={2} id="earned">When a row is earned</Heading>
  <p>
    A new tab should be paid for by cards that already exist, never by anticipation. Concretely:
  </p>
  <ul class="rules">
    <li>
      <strong>Three or four cards have made the same complaint.</strong> Not one card that fits
      badly — a repeated near-miss, in the same direction, over weeks. One awkward card is a
      card. Four are a pattern.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>You can name the discipline it keeps colliding with.</strong> If you cannot say
      which existing row nearly worked, you have not found a gap; you have found a card you have
      not thought about hard enough yet.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The new row and the old row split on a real question.</strong> Ideally the
      mechanism-or-action question that
      <a href={`${base}/find`}>settles most ties</a> — that split holds up for decades, where a
      split on vocabulary does not.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>You would cut a physical tab for it today.</strong> If the answer is "not yet,
      there are only two cards", then it is not yet. Note it and carry on.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Until then, the near-miss goes <em>on the card</em>: file it in the closest row you can
    defend and write what did not fit in the margin. That margin note is the only record of the
    complaint, and it is what turns four vague frustrations into one obvious new row.
  </p>

  <Heading level={2} id="room">The room you have</Heading>
  <p>
    A code is <span class="ad">drawer</span> + <span class="ad">discipline</span> +
    <span class="ad">sub-discipline</span> — one digit, then two, then one. Two digits for the
    discipline is the scheme's central trade, and this is what it bought: a drawer holds{' '}
    {PER_DRAWER} disciplines, and the canonical cut spends {spentShare}% of them.
  </p>
  <table class="room">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Drawer</th>
        <th class="n">Disciplines used</th>
        <th class="n">Disciplines free</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {
        room.map((r) => (
          <tr>
            <td>
              <span class="ad">{r.code}</span> {r.name}
            </td>
            <td class="n">{r.used}</td>
            <td class="n">{r.free}</td>
          </tr>
        ))
      }
      <tr class="tot">
        <td>Total</td>
        <td class="n">{stats.divisions}</td>
        <td class="n">{totalFree.toLocaleString('en')}</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p><strong>Every discipline gets nine sub-disciplines.</strong></p>

  <p class="meta">
    There are also four unused drawers, <span class="ad">6000</span> to
    {' '}<span class="ad">9000</span>, which you will almost certainly never need, for reasons
    further down.
  </p>

  <Heading level={2} id="how">Three ways to add a row, in order of preference</Heading>

  <Heading level={3} class="way" id="sub-discipline">1. A sub-discipline under an existing discipline</Heading>
  <p>
    Take the next free units digit under the discipline it belongs to.
    <span class="ad">5050</span> Medicine and health becomes the parent of
    <span class="ad">5051</span>, <span class="ad">5052</span>, and so on up to nine. This is
    the move you want, nearly always:
  </p>
  <ul class="rules">
    <li>It cannot collide with anything canonical, because the core list leaves every units digit empty.</li>
    <li>It sharpens a tiebreak that already exists rather than inventing a new one — the parent's scope note already told you what competes with it.</li>
    <li>It survives a change to the core list, because the first three digits are not yours and you did not touch them.</li>
    <li>It reads correctly to anyone who knows the scheme: a non-zero fourth digit <em>is</em> the notation for "a sub-discipline of the row above me".</li>
  </ul>

  <Heading level={3} class="way" id="new-discipline">2. A new discipline in a free tens slot</Heading>
  <p>
    Legal, and there are {totalFree} slots free, so you will not run out. But understand what
    you are doing: a discipline is a claim about how the field is cut, and you are now making one
    inside the numbering space the canonical list uses. Two consequences worth accepting on
    purpose:
  </p>
  <ul class="rules">
    <li>
      <strong>Mark it as yours.</strong> A dot in the corner of the guide card is enough. When
      you compare your box against the published list a year from now, you want to know
      instantly which tabs you invented.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Leave a gap.</strong> Number yours from the end of the drawer's used range with a
      few tens to spare, so a later core addition and your row do not want the same code.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    And before you do it: check that what you have is not a sub-discipline of something. Most
    candidates for a new discipline turn out to be the fourth digit of an existing one, which is
    the same row with less risk.
  </p>

  <Heading level={3} class="way" id="new-drawer">3. A new drawer</Heading>
  <p>
    Four first digits are unused, so this is possible. It is also almost always a mistake. The
    five drawers are not subject areas — they are five <em>kinds of knowing</em>: what people
    made, how people behave together, how the physical world works, what holds in any world, and
    what to do on Monday. A sixth kind is a genuine philosophical claim, and if you have one, you
    disagree with the core cut rather than extending it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The one honest use is a drawer that is not knowledge at all — a personal archive, papers,
    correspondence. Those are not cards in the same box and giving them
    <span class="ad">9000</span> is reasonable. Keep them out of the taxonomy either way.
  </p>

  <Heading level={2} id="scope">Write the scope note in two halves</Heading>
  <p>
    A row without a scope note is just a word, and a word does not settle anything at two in the
    afternoon with a card in your hand. Every row on the core list has a note in the same shape,
    and yours should too:
  </p>
  <div class="recipe">
    <p class="half"><span>First half</span> the nouns that file here — enough of them to be recognisable, not a definition.</p>
    <p class="half"><span>Second half</span> the tiebreak: name the discipline that competes, and say which side of the line each is on.</p>
    <p class="eg">
      <span class="ad">5051</span> Nutrition and metabolic health — <em>Diet, fasting, keto,
      macronutrients, and metabolic protocols as practice. How a metabolism works is 3050.</em>
    </p>
  </div>
  <p>
    The second half is the half that does the work, and it is the half people skip. Writing it
    forces you to answer the question you will actually face — not <em>what is this row</em> but
    <em>why this row and not that one</em> — and it is why the table can be read at a glance
    instead of studied.
  </p>
  <p>
    Name the competing code explicitly. A note that says "not the theoretical side" ages badly;
    a note that says "the theory is <span class="ad">4070</span>" is a pointer you can follow
    when you have forgotten your own reasoning.
  </p>

  <Heading level={2} id="never">What not to do</Heading>
  <ul class="rules dont">
    <li>
      <strong>Do not renumber a core code.</strong> Not to make room, not to close a gap, not
      for tidiness. Every card that points at a discipline points at the number, and the whole
      value of the scheme is that a pointer stays true forever. Gaps cost nothing; renumbering
      costs everything.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Do not push meaning past the fourth digit.</strong> Everything after the slash is
      position, not classification. If you find yourself deciding that
      <span class="ad">5051/3</span> should mean fasting specifically, stop — you are rebuilding
      the taxonomy inside the branch, and the branch cannot bear it.
      <a href={`${base}/rules`}>Why →</a>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Do not divide by project, source, or date.</strong> Those are true of a card for a
      year and useless for the next thirty. A card's discipline should be the last thing about it
      that changes.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Do not add a row for one card.</strong> The card is not homeless; it is
      inconvenient. File it in the nearest defensible row, note the near-miss, and let it wait
      for company.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Do not fork the whole table.</strong> If you re-cut the drawers you lose the
      ability to compare notes with anyone using the published list, and you gain nothing that a
      fourth digit would not have given you.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <Heading level={2} id="separate">Keep yours visibly separate</Heading>
  <p>
    Two tiers, on purpose: the core list can be handed to a stranger, and additions can arrive
    for years without a canonical code moving. That property is worth maintaining in your own
    box too — mark your guide cards, and keep a list of what you added and what earned it.
  </p>
  <p>
    This site does it by tagging every row in one file as core or personal, which is why
    {' '}<a href={`${base}/core`}>one page</a> can be canonical while
    {' '}<a href={`${base}/extensions`}>another</a> is openly one person's. The
    {' '}<a href={`${base}/find`}>finder searches both</a>, because when you are holding a card
    you want every row you have, not only the respectable ones.
  </p>
  <div class="test">
    <p>
      <strong>Then go and read the worked example.</strong> {personal.length} sub-disciplines,
      each with the card that would not file anywhere else and the discipline it kept colliding
      with. The rows will not be yours; the reasoning is the part to copy.
      <a href={`${base}/extensions`}>My extensions →</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .hero {
    padding: 3.5rem 0 0;
  }
  .hero h1 {
    margin: 0.5rem 0 1rem;
  }
  .tldr {
    margin: 0.6rem 0 0;
    padding-left: 1.2rem;
    font-size: 0.93rem;
  }
  .tldr li {
    margin-bottom: 0.3rem;
  }
  .rules {
    padding-left: 1.2rem;
  }
  .rules li {
    margin-bottom: 0.7rem;
  }
  .dont li strong {
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .test,
  .recipe {
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--brass);
    border-radius: 3px;
    padding: 0.9rem 1.1rem;
    margin: 1.2rem 0;
  }
  .test p,
  .recipe p {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: none;
  }
  .recipe .half {
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }
  .recipe .half span {
    display: inline-block;
    min-width: 6.6rem;
    font: 600 0.7rem/1.6 var(--sans);
    letter-spacing: 0.07em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--brass);
  }
  .recipe .eg {
    margin-top: 0.8rem;
    padding-top: 0.7rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
    font: 0.86rem/1.5 var(--serif);
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  .room {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: 0.87rem;
    margin: 1.1rem 0;
  }
  .room th {
    text-align: left;
    font: 600 0.7rem/1.4 var(--sans);
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--muted);
    padding-bottom: 0.4rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .room td {
    padding: 0.4rem 0.6rem 0.4rem 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .room .n {
    text-align: right;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    font-family: var(--mono);
    padding-right: 0;
  }
  .room th.n {
    text-align: right;
  }
  .room .tot td {
    font-weight: 600;
    border-bottom: 0;
  }
  .meta {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
  }
  /* :global — rendered by Heading.astro, outside this page's style scope, so
     the class has to be one no other page uses. */
  :global(.way) {
    margin-top: 1.6rem;
  }
</style>
